{"task":"triaj concerns: bryce-m5.local","goals":"Repo-level concerns with no commit to hang on","status":"Waiting","concerns_owner":"bryce-m5.local","standalone_concerns":[{"id":"cn-b3814414bca7","text":"persite mode gives every site the same Caddy admin port, so the second site cannot start","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-08T11:20:51.761Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-08T11:37:49.827Z","resolved_by":"476ad4b44be9afecbeaabb1ca92c8236a379ab27","resolved_by_actor":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad"},{"id":"cn-c00f8ddf0002","text":"Per-site mode has no way to run a site as a root daemon, so port 443 is unreachable by design\n\npersite runs each site as a per-user LaunchAgent, and on macOS an unprivileged process cannot bind a specific address on a port below 1024. So a persite site can serve 443 to the whole network, or not serve 443 at all — it cannot serve 443 to loopback. The bare https://<site>.<tld> URL is therefore unavailable in the mode, with no hint from the tool that this is why. Restoring it for matsu.ldev needed a hand-written LaunchDaemon plist and a swap script kept outside the repo in ~/.config/ldev/matsu-443. If that is worth supporting, ldev new (or an ldev daemon <site> subcommand) could emit the daemon plist alongside the Caddyfile and file the one privileged step, instead of each site being rebuilt by hand.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T10:59:46.936Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[]}]}
